{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-109673",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y=2{X}_{1}-3{X}_{2} & ∼N(2{\\mu }_{1}-3{\\mu }_{2},{2}^{2}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(20-15,4\\cdot 16+9\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ∼N(5,100).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =5$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{100}=10.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(Y\\ge 10).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 25) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{10-5}{10}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.5) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.5) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.5)≈0.6915.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 10) & =1-Φ(0.5) \\\\  & =1-0.6915 \\\\  & ≈0.31.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
